If $matbold(A)$ preserves dot products, then it preserves norms by taking $vb(x) = vb(y)$. Conversely, if $matbold(A) in O(n)$, the polarisation identity applied to $matbold(A) vb(x)$ and $matbold(A) vb(y)$ gives
$
  2 (matbold(A) vb(x)) dot (matbold(A) vb(y)) = 2 vb(x) dot vb(y).
$
Thus the two conditions are equivalent.
